WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.040
Short haul air tickets often exceed the jet fuel cost per passenger by a factor of 10 or more. This is First Principles Daily, episode 124. A typical domestic short-held ticket can run well above the direct cost of moving one passenger through the air. The jet fuel alone required for that leg often sits far lower than the price paid at the gate. That spread points to layers of process and constraint that sit on top of the basic physics of flight.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.800
Airlines schedule flights around scarce airport slots that are allocated through long-standing

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.220
rules and negotiations. Those slots limit how many planes can depart in a given hour,

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.440
so carriers protect the ones they hold by filling them even when marginal revenue is thin.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:52.000
Distribution systems add another layer. Global distribution services and online travel agencies

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:57.380
take fees that can reach double-digit percentages of the fare. Yield management algorithms

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:00.800
than adjust prices in real-time to protect high-value seats.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.540
The same physical seat can sell for widely different amounts on the same flight.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.800
Crew costs are fixed once the flight is scheduled,

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.900
yet they are spread across a passenger count that varies with load factor.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:19.100
Airport charges for landing, parking, and terminal use are set by contracts that change slowly

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.420
and often include minimum guarantees.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:26.080
Maintenance reserves and insurance are calculated on fleet-wide averages

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:28.080
rather than per flight marginal wear.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.240
The result is that the visible ticket price bundles

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.560
many costs that do not scale directly

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.060
with one additional passenger.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.500
Inside the industry, these markups feel normal

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.400
because every competitor faces the same slot rules,

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:43.480
the same distribution contracts,

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.660
and the same regulatory framework.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.720
Changing any one piece requires coordination

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.860
across airports, regulators, and booking platforms

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.120
that have evolved together over decades.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:59.420
rates. The baseline price therefore reflects accumulated decisions about risk, capacity,

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:05.480
and revenue protection more than the kilograms of fuel burned. One objection sometimes raised

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:11.340
is that fuel prices fluctuate and can occasionally dominate short routes. Yet even when fuel is

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:17.160
at recent commodity levels, the non-fuel components remain the larger share because slot scarcity

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:22.460
and distribution contracts do not shrink with lower fuel prices. Another common point

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:28.440
is that crew and maintenance are real expenses, but these are largely fixed once the schedule is set,

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:34.060
and therefore do not explain why adding one more passenger still carries a high incremental price.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:39.740
The structure persists because each layer, slot allocation, distribution contracts,

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:46.100
yield rules, and airport fees, has its own entrenched incentives that resist isolated change.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:51.580
If every structural and procedural step could be removed and the aircraft simply loaded

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:57.140
with the exact fuel needed for one passenger, the floor would be set by the energy content of

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:03.000
that fuel, plus minor engine and airframe wear. Jet fuel energy density is well known,

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:09.380
and the lift-to-drag ratio of a modern narrow body is also documented in public aerodynamic data.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:15.420
A rough calculation therefore starts with the aircraft empty weight, adds the passenger and baggage

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:21.240
mass, then multiplies by the fuel burn per kilometer at cruise altitude. The resulting

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:26.820
figure for a typical 500 kilometer leg lands in the low tens of dollars per passenger,

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:32.760
when fuel is priced at recent commodity levels. That number is only an estimate because actual

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:38.680
burned varies with winds, routing, and aircraft age. Adding the marginal cost of engine time

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:44.130
between overholes and the small fraction of airframe life consumed per flight raises the

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:47.870
floor modestly, but still keeps it well below observed fairs.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:53.490
The idiot index is then the ratio of the average paid ticket to this estimated material

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:54.490
and energy floor.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:04:00.430
Even allowing wide error bars on both sides, the ratio remains high enough to show that

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.250
the dominant costs lie outside the fuel itself.

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:08.830
The gap opens first at the airport authority level, where slot scarcity and infrastructure

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:13.710
structure charges are set by administrative processes rather than continuous competition.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:19.450
It widens again in the booking channel where each intermediary takes a cut that is independent

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:20.850
of the physics of the flight.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:27.070
Carrying costs compound because aircraft and crews must be positioned hours in advance,

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:30.670
and any delay multiplies payroll and lease expenses.

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:36.550
Fragmented maintenance supply chains and regulatory approval cycles for new procedures add

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:41.510
further layers that do not shrink when one more passenger boards. Each of these stages

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:46.190
can be quantified in principle by subtracting the fuel component from published airline

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:52.150
cost breakdowns, though exact allocations differ by carrier and route. One objection

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:57.810
is that the floor estimate ignores the capital cost of the aircraft itself. Yet that capital

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:03.470
cost is already amortized across thousands of flights and therefore contributes only a small

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:07.210
increment to any single short haul leg once the schedule exists.

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:13.730
Another objection is that safety margins require extra fuel reserves, but those reserves are

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:18.550
still measured in kilograms of jet fuel and remain within the same order as the cruise

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:21.790
burn rather than explaining the full gap to the ticket price.

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:27.730
A redesign would begin by asking what minimum infrastructure is required to move a standardized

00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:31.010
aluminum tube between two points at cruise speed.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:36.950
That question immediately surfaces slot allocation and terminal design as the first targets.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:42.350
If airports could dynamically price and trade departure windows in smaller increments,

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:45.230
the carrying cost of empty seats might fall.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:47.310
Mikes would come distribution.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:53.210
A direct carrier to passenger channel that bypasses legacy reservation systems could remove

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:55.030
several percentage points of fare.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:06:02.110
Manufacturing scale enters when airframe builders move from low-rate, custom assembly to higher volume lines

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:07.110
that treat the fuselage as a commodity pressure vessel rather than a bespoke structure.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:14.110
Policy changes would be required to allow denser seating standards or simplified certification for proven derivatives.

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:20.430
The genuinely hard constraints are safety certification timelines, the physics of wake turbulence

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:25.330
that limits runway throughput and the capital intensity of new airport capacity.

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:30.410
Any attempt to compress those steps must still satisfy certification authorities

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:35.170
that existing margins on structural loads and engine reliability remain intact.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:38.930
The prize is visible once the fuel floor is isolated.

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:45.390
Even a partial compression of the non-fuel layers would shift the economics of routes that are currently marginal.

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:53.230
One practical objection is that weak turbulent separation standards are set by physics and cannot be removed by policy.

00:06:53.970 --> 00:07:02.590
Yet the same standards apply to every operator and therefore do not prevent relative cost reductions if slot trading and distribution fees are addressed first.

00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:14.210
Another objection is that certification authorities move slowly by design, but the redesign path can still target areas outside certifications such as booking channels and slot markets,

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:18.210
while certification work proceeds in parallel on narrower changes.

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:24.590
One principle is that when a service price is set mainly by allocation rules and intermediary fees,

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:28.450
the physical resource itself becomes a small share of the total.

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:33.870
Another is that the first redesign step is always to separate the energy cost of emotion

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:36.450
from the institutional cost of coordination.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:41.370
Tomorrow, the show returns with another concrete case or another domain

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:43.450
where the same separation can be made.

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:48.550
Who will be the first to publish a transparent per passenger fuel plus wear ledger

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:50.570
for a specific short haul route?

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:53.810
That's a wrap on today's first Principles Breakdown.

